## TODO: nexus-prototype-merge

Phase 1 of the multi-source expansion. The phases must land in this order:
nexus-prototype-merge -> source-identity-refactor -> mod-source-domain-model ->
sources-module-restructure -> acquire-install-split -> index-multi-source.
kill-pd3-fallback is independent and can land any time.

### Context and reasoning (read before implementing)

Goal of the whole track: expand Modrex from one source (modworkshop) to many
(Nexus Mods, mod.io, Steam Workshop, ModDB) across many games. A full architecture
review (2026-07-03) found the app has no domain model of "a mod": the modworkshop
wire format IS the data model, and the modworkshop numeric id IS mod identity, baked
into the persisted state file, every cache, the IPC surface, the dependency system,
and the SHA256 index. The install engine BELOW the "archive on disk" boundary
(zip.rs, ScanTargets, sentinels, state management) is already source-agnostic and
must not be restructured by this work.

Sequencing decision, deliberate: the branch `feature/nexus-prototype` merges into
main BEFORE the deep refactor, not after. Merging after is the worst ordering: the
refactor rewrites exactly the files the branch touches (types.rs, identify.rs,
api.ts, App.tsx, installedUtils.ts), so every refactor commit conflicts with the
branch, and the final "merge" is a rewrite anyway. Merging first is cheap (the
branch is mostly additive new files) and makes the later refactor better: the
source abstraction gets extracted from two live implementations instead of one.

### Facts about feature/nexus-prototype (verified against the branch)

- Forked from 0.12.1 (commit 003a684). One commit, f61e99a (skip startup update
  check in dev builds), was already cherry-picked to main - expect it to dedupe or
  trivially conflict when merging.
- New Rust modules: `commands/nexus.rs` (+tests) - API client with GraphQL mods
  search; `commands/nxm.rs` (+tests) - nxm:// deep-link parsing and handoff
  (case-insensitive domain match, secrets kept out of logs). New renderer page:
  `NexusBrowsePage.tsx` (separate from BrowsePage, deliberately). Tauri deep-link
  plugin config + ACL/schema changes in `tauri.conf.json`, `lib.rs`, `gen/schemas`.
- Identity convention on the branch: a Nexus install gets
  `uid = "nexus:{nexus_mod_id}:{nexus_file_id}"`, `id = -(nexus_mod_id)`,
  `source = "nexus"`, plus new optional state fields `author` and `thumbnail_url`
  (only recorded for non-modworkshop sources). THE UID ENCODES THE FULL REAL
  IDENTITY - this is the invariant that makes the later identity refactor a
  mechanical state migration. Never break it for anything new persisted.
- Guarded special cases that exist on the branch and are DELETED later by the
  refactor (do not multiply them): identify.rs skips nexus entries in the fuzzy
  name fallback and name-suffix regroup (SHA256 cross-post upgrade still allowed);
  computeHealthSummary excludes nexus entries from "unidentified"; syntheticMod
  renders author/thumbnail from state.
- `GAMES` gained optional `nexusDomain` (pd3 = "payday3",
  cb = "crimebossrockaycity").
- install_nexus_download reuses resolve_archive_download + install_mod_from_path;
  the four picker sentinels (ZIP_MULTI_PAK / HOST_MOD_PACK / CB_FLAT_ARCHIVE /
  UNRECOGNIZED_ARCHIVE) currently FAIL with an honest error ("needs a manual
  install choice that Nexus downloads don't support yet") because the picker
  modals are modworkshop-shaped.
- Nexus platform constraints the branch encodes: free-tier users cannot get API
  download links (premium only) - downloads arrive via the site's "Mod Manager
  Download" button firing an nxm:// link with a signed key; hence the deep-link
  subsystem. API key is entered by the user in Settings (prototype: stored in
  settings.json plaintext).

### What to do

1. Merge main into the branch (or rebase) to clear drift; repeat periodically
   while work continues. Keep new branch work additive (new files) wherever
   possible.
2. Pre-merge polish, in the branch:
   - Decide the sentinel story: either keep the honest failure for v1 (acceptable,
     it is explicit and rare) or wire ZipPickerModal for nexus installs. Do NOT
     silently guess an install target.
   - Decide on API key storage. Plaintext settings.json is a known tradeoff; if
     kept, say so in the Settings UI copy. (A proper secrets story can ride the
     sources-module-restructure phase.)
   - Nexus feature stays naturally gated: no API key configured -> no Nexus UI.
     Verify that holds everywhere (Sidebar, Settings, deep-link handler).
3. Merge to main. Conventional commit; user runs all remote git commands.
4. Verify end-to-end after merge: nxm:// handoff from the Nexus website installs a
   real PD3 mod and a real Crime Boss mod; entries show in InstalledPage with
   author + thumbnail; enable/disable/uninstall work; modworkshop flows unchanged.

