## TODO: acquire-install-split

Phase 5 of the multi-source expansion.

### Context

`download.rs::download_file` assumes "a direct HTTPS GET yields the archive". That
holds for modworkshop and premium Nexus only. The install pipeline below "archive
on disk" is source-agnostic and stays untouched.

### What to do

- Split acquisition from installation: an acquisition step per source produces
  "an archive/folder on disk" and hands it to the existing install engine.
  Strategies: plain HTTP GET (modworkshop, mod.io), nxm:// deep-link handoff
  (Nexus free tier - already built on the branch), steamcmd / manage-in-place
  (Steam Workshop, future), browser handoff (last resort).
- Steam Workshop note for whenever it lands: content is NOT archives over HTTP.
  Metadata via IPublishedFileService web API (needs a Steam Web API key);
  delivery via the Steam client (subscribe + ISteamUGC) or
  `steamcmd workshop_download_item` (anonymous works only for some games). For
  many games "install" means "manage what Steam already downloaded" - a different
  install model, likely a new ScanTarget-like concept pointing at the workshop
  content dir rather than a copy step.
- ModDB note: no public API; scraping only, behind Cloudflare bot management that
  defeats library HTTP clients (see news.rs, which had to shell out to curl.exe).
  Deliberately LAST or never.
- Recommended source order after Nexus: mod.io next (API-first, direct binary
  downloads, OAuth or API key, designed for managers - validates the abstraction
  with the least incidental complexity), then Steam Workshop, then ModDB.
  Re-verify Nexus and mod.io API terms (registered-app requirements, free-user
  download policy) against current documents before building - the architecture
  above holds either way, but product constraints may have changed.

---

## TODO: index-multi-source

Phase 6 of the multi-source expansion. Work spans modrex-index and modrex-main.

### Context

index.db already has a `sources` table (schema: games -> sources -> mods ->
files), but modrex-main's queries (mod_index.rs) filter by game name only and
return a bare remote id the app assumes is a modworkshop id. Cross-posting is
common (authors publish the same file to Nexus + mod.io + modworkshop), so one
SHA256 will match multiple identities.

### What to do

- mod_index.rs lookups return `(source, remote_id)`; callers stop assuming
  modworkshop. Precedence policy for multi-source SHA256 hits: prefer the source
  the mod was installed from, else a fixed priority order.
- modrex-index gains per-source fetchers. Constraint: the "download everything
  and hash SHA256" strategy is modworkshop-shaped. Nexus ToS will not tolerate
  mass-downloading, but Nexus offers an MD5 lookup API - identification there
  wants MD5, which means per-source identification strategies and possibly
  dual-hashing (SHA256 + MD5) installed files in modrex-main going forward.
  Steam Workshop hashing would require steamcmd-driven downloads.
- Keep the hard rule from builder-state: zero-yield mods never become `mods` rows
  (query_by_name uniqueness depends on it), per source.

---

## TODO: kill-pd3-fallback

Independent of the multi-source phases; do before scaling past a handful of games.

### Context

`engine_for_game` maps any unknown game id to `&PD3_ENGINE`; `game_def_for_id`
does the same; nearly every Tauri command starts with
`game_id.as_deref().unwrap_or("pd3")`. With 4 games and disciplined call sites it
is survivable; with many games, a typo'd or missing gameId silently reads/writes
PD3's directories - a data-corruption class of bug.

### What to do

- `engine_for_game` and `game_def_for_id` return Result/Option; unknown game id is
  a hard command error, never a silent PD3.
- Retire the `Option<String> game_id` backward-compat defaults on the IPC surface:
  make gameId required in api.ts wrappers and in the Rust command signatures. The
  renderer always has `activeGame`; the Option-ness only exists for pre-multi-game
  compat.
- Sweep: `unwrap_or("pd3")` across src-tauri/src/commands/ (mods/mod.rs,
  launchers/mod.rs, settings.rs, news.rs).
- Note for the far future, do not act now: games are compile-time Rust statics +
  a TS union (`GameId`, `GAMES`, engine.rs statics, launchers/games/*.rs). Each
  new game is code + a release, and history shows each game needed bespoke
  subsystems anyway (crimeboss_settings.rs, pdmod.rs, host_mods.rs,
  ue4ss_modstxt.rs) - roughly 60% config, 40% reverse-engineering. A data-driven
  game registry only pays off if game count grows past what hand-written modules
  sustain; revisit then, not preemptively.

